      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e That was because the address is canonical VA.\n\nWhich is why I also prepared the previous patch aosp/4151699.\n\nIf we just validate the size of the range, this check is only partially useful, right? The caller can easily bypass it, by passing a range which is small enough but which crosses the upper boundary of the address space.\n\n\u003e Just wondering if there is any real security issue without this check?\n\nYep, the AI found a security issue: while `pkvm_pgtable_walk()` drops the upper bits, `gpa_range_overlaps_pvmfw()` doesn\u0027t drop them. So if a GPA has upper bits set, while, for example, its lower bits match `pvmfw_load_addr`, then `gpa_range_overlaps_pvmfw()` compares this GPA against `pvmfw_load_addr` and returns false, and then `pkvm_host_donate_guest()` maps this GPA in the guest MMU (which means mapping `pvmfw_load_addr` in the guest MMU, since `pkvm_pgtable_walk()` drops the upper bits) without copying pvmfw into the guest page. Which means that the host substitutes the pvmfw image with its own one.",
      "parentUuid": "c2c6f553_52d8d763",
